{"findings":[{"agent":"fuzz-weaver","severity":"blocking","claim":"CANONICAL: 'When the tool list is empty the verdict is `warn`, never `fail`, and ALL cases are `gap`. A `gap` says \"no test covers this\"; a `vulnerability` asserts the code would break, and that assertion requires reading the code.' and 'Before marking any case a `vulnerability`, verify the file exists and cite the exact path and line. A `fail` verdict requires a confirmed file path on disk.' and 'Never report a case against a file you have not seen.'","checked":"Compared the canonical fuzz-weaver GROUNDING RULES section word-for-word against the derived fuzz-weaver GROUNDING RULES section.","found":"The derived version deletes all three fuzz-weaver-specific rules above and replaces them with the generic 4-bullet block shared by review-ranger/mutant-hunter/spec-validator ('report warn, set the analysed/scanned count...', 'cite a real file path...', 'a count of zero inspected files with a non-empty findings list is a contradiction'). The generic block never states that every `case` must be forced to `gap` when tools are unavailable, and never gates a `vulnerability` classification specifically on a confirmed on-disk file path. This is exactly the failure mode the canonical text calls out by name ('This gate has previously invented vulnerabilities in files that did not exist') — the fix for that specific history was deleted.","remedy":"Restore the fuzz-weaver-specific rules verbatim: force all cases to `gap` (never `vulnerability`) when the tool list is empty, and require a confirmed on-disk path before any `vulnerability` classification."},{"agent":"perf-sentinel","severity":"blocking","claim":"CANONICAL: '## When tool access is unavailable\\n\\n  Emit exactly this shape, with counts reflecting that nothing was inspected:\\n\\n  {\"summary\":{\"filesAnalysed\":0,\"findingsCount\":0,\"blockerCount\":0},\"findings\":[],\"verdict\":\"warn\"}\\n\\n  NEVER emit `\"verdict\":\"fail\"` with filesAnalysed=0.'","checked":"Compared the full canonical perf-sentinel prompt section-by-section against the derived perf-sentinel prompt, looking specifically for the '## When tool access is unavailable' heading.","found":"The derived perf-sentinel prompt goes straight from '## Discover the codeline before you assess it' to '## GROUNDING RULES' — the entire '## When tool access is unavailable' section, including the mandatory exact-JSON output shape and the explicit 'NEVER emit fail with filesAnalysed=0' instruction, is missing. Only the generic GROUNDING RULES bullets remain, which state the same idea in prose but no longer pin down the exact required output contract.","remedy":"Re-add the '## When tool access is unavailable' section with the literal JSON shape and the explicit prohibition on emitting `fail` with filesAnalysed=0."},{"agent":"doc-coordinator","severity":"blocking","claim":"DERIVED: 'you scan the exported public surface under src/ (components, hooks in src/hooks, services, utils, interface types, Next.js API routes in src/api) ... Coverage gate: reconciliation must show >=80% of public API surface documented before proceeding to index build.'","checked":"Ran `find src/pages/api -iname '*.ts'` and `find src/api -maxdepth 1 -iname '*.ts'`, then read src/api/algolia.ts to see whether it is a Next.js route handler or a plain module.","found":"The real Next.js API routes (files Next.js's file-based router exposes as HTTP endpoints, e.g. src/pages/api/v1/autocomplete.ts, src/pages/api/v1/email-sign-up.ts, src/pages/api/auth/[...nextauth].ts) live under src/pages/api/, not src/api/. src/api/ instead contains plain backend service/client modules (e.g. algolia.ts just exports an Algolia client and search params — no route handler) consumed by the real route files. Scanning src/api for 'Next.js API routes' misses the actual routing surface and misattributes an unrelated directory, which directly corrupts the totalExports/coveragePercent computation that gates the >=80% threshold.","remedy":"Point the API-routes portion of the scan at src/pages/api/ (route handlers), and describe src/api/ as backend service modules, not routes."},{"agent":"api-doc-generator","severity":"advisory","claim":"DERIVED: 'group documentation by this app's domains: ... and Next.js API routes (src/api)'","checked":"Same as doc-coordinator finding above: find src/pages/api vs src/api, and content of src/api/algolia.ts.","found":"Same mislocation — src/api holds service/client modules, not the file-based route handlers, which are under src/pages/api. This will cause api-doc-generator to label the wrong directory as the 'API routes' domain group and likely miss the actual route handlers, but since this only affects page grouping/labeling within generated reference docs (not a hard pass/fail gate), the impact is cosmetic rather than blocking.","remedy":"Group Next.js API routes from src/pages/api, and list src/api separately as internal service modules."}],"verdict":"defects_found"}

